From: Stanislaw Pal <kuncy7@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 20:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] net: dsa: qca8k, rtl8365mb: 8021q taggers: a VID belongs to
 a VLAN-aware bridge only

The qca-8021q and rtl8365mb-8021q taggers deliver a frame that carries a
VLAN of a VLAN-aware bridge by the VID alone, so one VID can serve one
bridge, and the drivers refuse a VLAN that another bridge already has.

Both the check and the delivery asked the bridge whether a port has the
VID configured, and a VLAN-unaware bridge answers yes as well: every
port of it holds the default PVID 1, which is not on the wire. So with an
ordinary br-lan on some ports of the switch

 - a port could not join a second, VLAN-aware bridge: its default PVID 1
   was "a VLAN of another VLAN-aware bridge" (-EBUSY, "failed to
   initialize vlan filtering on this port");
 - a frame in VID 1 of a VLAN-aware bridge could be handed to a port of
   the VLAN-unaware one, whichever came first in the port list.

Counting VLAN-aware bridges only is not enough on its own. What decided
whether a bridge VLAN went into the VLAN table was the port's state when
the VLAN was added and the VLAN's flags:

 - a VLAN added tagged to a port of a VLAN-unaware bridge was taken for
   the VLAN of an 802.1Q upper and installed;
 - the VLANs of a bridge that turned VLAN filtering on later were never
   installed, and those of one that turned it off stayed;
 - a delete was passed on for a VLAN that had never been installed.

With the first check relaxed, each of these lets two bridges end up in
one VLAN table entry.

So keep track, per user port, of what the driver did put into the table
- the VLANs of the port's VLAN-aware bridge, and those of its 802.1Q
uppers - and work from that:

 - a VLAN of a bridge is told from one of an 802.1Q upper by
   obj.orig_dev, which the bridge sets and the core's upper path does
   not, and stays in software while the bridge is VLAN-unaware;
 - a VID is taken by another bridge when it is in another port's map,
   not when a bridge has it configured;
 - a delete removes what the map says is there and nothing else;
 - turning VLAN filtering on checks the bridge's VLANs - all of them,
   over all of its ports, so that the first port asked refuses before
   anything has changed - and installs the port's; turning it off, or
   leaving the bridge, removes them.

On the receive side a new helper next to
dsa_find_designated_bridge_port_by_vid() looks at ports of VLAN-aware
bridges only.

Found while testing ADCDS's an8855-8021q series, whose tagger and owner
check already look at VLAN-aware ports only.

Signed-off-by: Stanislaw Pal <kuncy7@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/dsa/qca/qca8k-8xxx.c
+++ b/drivers/net/dsa/qca/qca8k-8xxx.c
@@ -2013,6 +2013,10 @@
 	if (proto == priv->tag_proto)
 		return 0;
 
+	/* What the 8021q mode kept track of is the other mode's from here on */
+	memset(priv->bridge_vlans, 0, sizeof(priv->bridge_vlans));
+	memset(priv->upper_vlans, 0, sizeof(priv->upper_vlans));
+
 	switch (proto) {
 	case DSA_TAG_PROTO_QCA:
 		dsa_tag_8021q_unregister(ds);
--- a/drivers/net/dsa/qca/qca8k-common.c
+++ b/drivers/net/dsa/qca/qca8k-common.c
